// A #local chain settles in ONE cycle. That is the whole reason #local exists:
// written as #variable rules the same chain takes one cycle per step, because
// each read sees the previous commit.
//
// It did not. csp.h's DECL_HEADER note describes the mechanism -- "its leaf is
// copied DOUT->DIN right after (so a read in the SAME cycle sees it -- see
// csp_local_commit)" -- and csp_local_commit was never written. The bit, the
// documentation and the manual all promised it; the copy was missing, so a
// #local behaved exactly like a #variable. lib/analog.csp has a chain seven
// deep, which put its output seven cycles behind its input.
#variable a = 0
#local s1 = a + 1
#local s2 = s1 * 2
#local s3 = s2 + 1

// The same chain as plain variables, for contrast: this one is EXPECTED to lag,
// and it is what #local is supposed to differ from.
// A #local has no name in the declaration table (it lives in the compiler's
// define buffer until #end) and no /state row, so the checks read it through
// mirrors. That does not weaken the test: m1 and m3 both lag the locals by
// exactly one cycle, so if the locals settle together the mirrors do too.
#variable m1 = 0
#variable m3 = 0

#variable v1 = 0
#variable v2 = 0
#variable v3 = 0
#variable n = 0

n = n + 1
a = 10 ? n > 1
m1 = s1
m3 = s3
v1 = a + 1
v2 = v1 * 2
v3 = v2 + 1
